% Origin: Kowalski & Calejo, "Teaching Logical Thinking through Logic
% Programming using Logical English, Argumentation Games and Animation",
% PEG Lisbon 2026, slide 25: "Abduction can be used to generate plans to
% achieve goals."
%
% The goal "alice likes you" is achieved by assuming the open (abducible)
% action "you wears sunglasses" — abduction as planning: the unknowns of the
% answer are the actions the plan requires. Read the answer with its unknowns
% as a conditional answer (slide 26): "alice likes you IF you wears
% sunglasses".

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a person* likes *a person*.
    *a person* looks cool.
    *a person* wears sunglasses; assumable.

the knowledge base sunglasses includes:

alice likes a person if the person looks cool.

a person looks cool if the person wears sunglasses.

scenario planning is:
    plan expects answers [
        "alice likes you"
    ] and unknowns [
        "you wears sunglasses"
    ].

query plan is:
    alice likes you.
